Title: The Velveteen Rabbit
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from book
Notes: One medium throughout — period live-action storybook, an English nursery of the 1920s; every toy practical, stitched, and handled.
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. NURSERY - MORNING

Frost ferns the window panes. A stocking hangs fat from the mantel, and wedged in its top sits THE RABBIT — a velveteen rabbit, fat and bunchy, his coat spotted brown and white, real thread whiskers, ears lined with pink sateen, a sprig of holly between his paws.
(SOUND: mantel clock ticking; church bells thin and far off in the cold)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was once a velveteen rabbit, and in the beginning he was really splendid.

THE BOY — six, nightshirt, tousled brown hair, bare feet — tips the stocking out onto the hearthrug: nuts, oranges, a toy engine, chocolate almonds, a clockwork mouse. He lifts the Rabbit up above all of it.
(SOUND: oranges thudding on the rug; the clockwork mouse whirring itself out)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For at least two hours the Boy loved him.

Aunts and uncles crowd in with parcels. Tissue paper storms the room. The Boy is drawn away by the hand. The Rabbit lies under a drift of paper, his holly sprig knocked askew.
(SOUND: rustling tissue and grown-up laughter swelling, then a door shutting on all of it)

INT. NURSERY - DAY

The toy cupboard stands open. On its shelves the mechanical toys are ranked in order, keys in their backs. A model boat with most of its paint gone. TIMOTHY, a jointed wooden lion, chin lifted. THE RABBIT sits on the floor below them, small.
(SOUND: clockwork whirring and clicking; a tin drum thumping by itself)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The mechanical toys were very superior, and looked down upon every one else; they were full of modern ideas, and pretended they were real. Between them all the poor little Rabbit was made to feel himself very insignificant and commonplace.

Beside the nursery fender lies THE SKIN HORSE — brown coat bald in patches, the seams showing underneath, his tail thin where the hairs have been pulled out to string bead necklaces. The Rabbit settles down side by side with him.
(SOUND: coals settling in the grate)

THE RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

THE SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you. When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

THE RABBIT
Does it hurt?

THE SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

THE RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

THE SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time. That's why it doesn't happen often to people who break easily, or have sharp edges, or who have to be carefully kept.

Firelight moves over his bald seams.
(MUSIC: one warm cello line held under the words)

THE SKIN HORSE
Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out and you get loose in the joints and very shabby. But these things don't matter at all, because once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

THE RABBIT
I suppose you are real?

The Skin Horse only smiles.

THE SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. That was a great many years ago; but once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

The Rabbit sighs, and looks down at his own bright, unworn paws.
(SOUND: brisk footsteps coming along the passage)

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

NANA — grey hair pinned up, starched apron, sleeves pushed back — goes through the room like a great wind, hustling playthings into cupboards.
(SOUND: cupboard doors banging; tin toys clattering; skirts sweeping the boards)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was a person called Nana who ruled the nursery. She called this "tidying up," and the playthings all hated it, especially the tin ones.

The Boy, in his nightshirt, kneels beside the bed, hunting under it. No china dog. Nana glances at the open cupboard and makes a swoop.

NANA
Here — take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

She drags the Rabbit out by one ear and puts him into the Boy's arms.

The night-light burns on the mantelpiece. Under the bedclothes the Boy raises a long tunnel with his knees and walks the Rabbit through it.
(SOUND: whispered laughter; sheets rustling; the small flame guttering)

THE BOY
(whispering)
That's a burrow. That's where the real rabbits live.

The Boy sleeps. The Rabbit is snugged beneath his warm chin, small hands clasped close round him.
(SOUND: slow breathing; the house gone entirely silent)

EXT. GARDEN - DAY

Spring. Blossom against the wall. The Boy trundles the Rabbit along the path in a wheelbarrow, then sets him inside a hut of twigs under the raspberry canes behind the flower border. Crumbs of a picnic on the grass. The Rabbit's velveteen is duller now, his tail beginning to unsew, the pink rubbed off his nose.
(SOUND: barrow wheel squeaking; bees; a wood pigeon in the elms)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Spring came, and wherever the Boy went the Rabbit went too. He never noticed how his beautiful velveteen fur was getting shabbier and shabbier.

A call from the house. The Boy runs off, cap in hand, leaving the Rabbit half-buried in the flower bed where he has been diving into burrows.

The light drains out of the garden. Dew beads on the velveteen. A candle flame bobs across the lawn — Nana, shawl over her nightdress, stooping and peering.
(SOUND: crickets; wet grass under her shoes; an exasperated sigh)

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

Nana rubs the earthy, soaking Rabbit with a corner of her apron.
(SOUND: cloth scrubbing on velveteen; the night-light hissing)

NANA
You must have your old Bunny! Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

The Boy sits up in bed and stretches out his hands.

THE BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

Close on the boot-button eyes that lost their polish long ago.
(MUSIC: a small bright figure on strings, held and rising)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The nursery magic had happened to him, and he was a toy no longer. He was Real. The Boy himself had said it.

Morning. Sun across the rumpled bed. Nana picks the Rabbit up off the floor, pauses, and looks properly at his face.

NANA
I declare if that old Bunny hasn't got quite a knowing expression!

EXT. WOOD - EVENING

June, after tea. Long light between the tree trunks. The Boy makes the Rabbit a nest in the bracken, then wanders off among the trees, playing at brigands. Ants run to and fro between the Rabbit's velvet paws.
(SOUND: bracken ticking in the breeze; the Boy whooping far off; insects in the grass)

Two beings creep out of the tall bracken — rabbits, but quite furry and brand-new, no seams showing, changing shape queerly as they move. THE FURRY RABBIT comes closest, nose twitching. A second wild rabbit hangs back.

THE FURRY RABBIT
Why don't you get up and play with us?

THE RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

THE FURRY RABBIT
Ho! It's as easy as anything.

He gives a big hop sideways and stands up on his hind legs.

THE FURRY RABBIT
I don't believe you can!

THE RABBIT
I can! I can jump higher than anything!

THE FURRY RABBIT
Can you hop on your hind legs?

The Rabbit sits very still in the bracken. The back of him is made all in one piece, like a pincushion.
(SOUND: soft padding feet coming closer through the leaf litter)

THE RABBIT
I don't want to!

The Furry Rabbit stretches out his neck and looks behind him.

THE FURRY RABBIT
He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs!

He laughs, and begins to whirl round and dance.

THE RABBIT
I have! I have got hind legs! I am sitting on them!

THE FURRY RABBIT
Then stretch them out and show me, like this!

The button eyes follow the dancing until the wood tilts and swims. A funny new tickly feeling runs all through the velveteen.

THE RABBIT
I don't like dancing. I'd rather sit still!

The Furry Rabbit stops. He comes so close that his long whiskers brush the Rabbit's ear — then wrinkles his nose, flattens his ears, jumps backwards.

THE FURRY RABBIT
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

THE RABBIT
I am Real! I am Real! The Boy said so!

Footsteps. The Boy runs past near them. A stamp of feet, a flash of white tails, and the bracken is empty.
(SOUND: drumming feet, then nothing but wind through the fronds)

THE RABBIT
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

No answer. Only the little ants running to and fro, and the bracken swaying where the strangers passed. The sun sinks lower. White moths flutter out. The Boy's hands lift him from the nest.
(MUSIC: a thin, lonely flute over the dusk)

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

Weeks later. The Rabbit is very old and shabby — his whiskers loved off, the pink lining of his ears turned grey, his brown spots faded, his shape
